% Implementation details for the deployed schedule of Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}.

\subsection{Targets, smoothing, and predictable staging}
\label{sec:adaptation-target-implementation}

The OU targets and the target pseudo-update period are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_\star&=c_\tau\frac{\widehat T_z}{2},\\
\widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}
 &=\sqrt{\max(\widehat\sigma_{\mathrm{wave}}^2,\epsilon)},\\
\sigma_{aw,\star}&=c_\sigma\widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star},\\
T_{S,\star}&=\clip(c_T\tau_\star,T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),\\
r_{S,\star}&=C_Jq_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B,\star}^{\,6/7}
 \tau_\star^{24/7}T_{S,\star}^{-1/2}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:tuner-targets}
\end{equation}
The last line is the deployed SpectralMSE law of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-default-rs-law}, with $C_J=0.0538$.  The implementation
uses the strong-observation reduced intensity $q_{\mathrm{eff}}\simeq2R_a h$
and caches its $1/14$ power.  Here $R_a$ is the separately configured
accelerometer measurement-noise variance used by the reduced SpectralMSE
model; its deployed default is $\sqrt{R_a}=\SI{0.0148}{m.s^{-2}}$.  It is not
the \SI{0.12}{m.s^{-2}} variance-channel noise floor subtracted by the online
tuner.  The two parameters therefore remain independently configurable even
though both describe acceleration uncertainty at different points in the
implementation.  The physical wave statistic $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$, rather
than $\sigma_{aw}$, enters the distortion term; in code the tuner supplies
$\sigma_{aw}=c_\sigma\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$ and the factor $c_\sigma$ is divided
back out when evaluating the regularizer target.

Applied-target candidates for $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ use a common EMA,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha&=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}},\\
\widetilde\tau&\leftarrow\widetilde\tau
 +\alpha(\tau_\star-\widetilde\tau),\\
\widetilde\sigma_{aw}&\leftarrow\widetilde\sigma_{aw}
 +\alpha(\sigma_{aw,\star}-\widetilde\sigma_{aw}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-ewma}
\end{equation}
with the sea-scaled horizon
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}=c_{\mathrm{adapt}}T_{\mathrm{sea}},\qquad
c_{\mathrm{adapt}}=0.40.
\label{eq:adapt-ema-sea-time}
\end{equation}
The dynamically estimated time base used for this EMA is guarded in
$[\SI{0.5}{s},\SI{6}{s}]$, and every dynamically generated EMA horizon is
finally guarded in $[\SI{0.05}{s},\SI{30}{s}]$.  These are safety envelopes,
not sea-state tuning points; all eight scored reference seas remain interior.

The regularizer candidate is smoothed separately,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_{r_S}&=m_{r_S}\tau_\star,\qquad
\alpha_{r_S}=1-e^{-\Delta t/\tau_{r_S}},\\
\widetilde r_S&\leftarrow
 \widetilde r_S
 +\alpha_{r_S}(r_{S,\star}-\widetilde r_S),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:adapt-RS}
\end{equation}
where the measured default is $m_{r_S}=1.5$ and the same universal dynamic-EMA
guard is applied to the realized horizon.  Every valid physical sample
contributes to the period statistic, acceleration statistic, and all adaptive
EMAs.

Candidate smoothing and parameter activation are distinct operations in the
source.  The candidate tuple
$\widetilde\vartheta=(\widetilde\tau,\widetilde\sigma_{aw},\widetilde r_S)$
is updated from every valid sample, but the active MEKF schedule is held between
activation instants.  The default activation timer is approximately
\SI{0.1}{s}.  When that timer expires after physical sample $k$, the current
candidate is marked pending; it is committed at the beginning of sample $k+1$,
before that sample's innovation.  Consequently
\begin{equation}
\vartheta_k=(\tau_k,\sigma_{aw,k},r_{S,k})\in\mathcal F_{k-1},
\label{eq:adapt-one-sample-staging}
\end{equation}
so the schedule remains predictable with respect to the current innovation,
but it is a sample-and-hold schedule rather than a new committed schedule every
\SI{5}{ms}.  At the nominal \SI{200}{Hz} IMU rate, the additional commit lag
\emph{after an activation instant} is one sample, approximately \SI{5}{ms}.
A second timer with the same approximately \SI{0.1}{s} default period performs
bounded latent-$a_w$ covariance maintenance on an independent clock; it does not
replace or bypass the parameter-activation gate.

The actual pseudo-update cadence follows the applied OU time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}),
\qquad
c_T=\frac{\SI{15}{ms}}{\SI{1.1}{s}},
\label{eq:engineering-pseudo-cadence-implementation}
\end{equation}
with $T_{S,\min}=\SI{5}{ms}$ and $T_{S,\max}=\SI{250}{ms}$.  For the deployed
SpectralMSE law, the $T_S^{-1/2}$ dependence is already contained in the target,
so the filter receives
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}=r_{S,\mathrm{appl}}
\qquad\text{(SpectralMSE default).}
\label{eq:engineering-rs-filter-implementation}
\end{equation}
No additional reference-cadence factor is applied.  Away from cadence clamps,
the deployed schedule therefore has
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
\propto\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\tau^{41/14},
\end{equation}
which is the reduced physical-MSE prediction used throughout the current
estimator results.

